+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+The Keel proportionality table lists "config values" on its lightest row, alongside docs,
+comments, and test-only fixes. Not every config value is inert, though: a one-line change to
+a Runtime timeout or to a permission setting changes runtime behaviour or security posture,
+yet under the current table it still routes to "nothing beyond the summary/task record" —
+no three-line card, no command that proves it, no behavioural evidence. That under-routes
+exactly the changes where "risk is read from what the change touches, not from how big it
+is," and it sits uneasily with the existing Journey-Proven Development proof rule that
+already requires behavioural evidence for runtime-affecting configuration (AGENTS.md,
+"Proof rules").
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Limit the light row of the Keel proportionality table to **inert** config values:
+  - `docs/process/DELIVERY.md`, section 2, first table row: "Docs, comments, config values, …"
+    becomes "Docs, comments, **inert** config values, …".
+  - `AGENTS.md`, "Keel: how code is written here", same row, same edit.
+- Add runtime- and security-affecting configuration to the **expanded** row of both tables
+  ("Persistence, permissions, compatibility, security, external effects"), so such a change
+  carries the full card and the JPD flow, including the behavioural-evidence proof rule.
+- Say the distinction plainly in both tables' surrounding prose: a config value counts as
+  inert only if it cannot affect runtime behaviour or security; any runtime- or
+  security-affecting config needs a full card and behavioural evidence.
+- Docs only: no checker, no `keel.yaml` rule, and no enforcement mechanism is added.
+- Recorded assumption (unattended run): "inert" is defined by the one-sentence test above
+  and is not formalized further; a repository-wide search confirms the "config values"
+  wording occurs in exactly these two tables, so no other document is edited.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `keel-card-routing`: the repository's routing of a change to its required Keel card level
+  (summary only, three-line card, full card, full card plus JPD flow) as a function of what
+  the change touches — including the inert vs. runtime-/security-affecting split for config
+  values.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+(none — `openspec/specs/` is empty; nothing exists to modify)
+
+## Impact
+
+- Two markdown files: `AGENTS.md` (Keel section) and `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (section 2).
+- No code, schemas, tooling, checkers, or tests are touched. No dependency notes.
+- Readers of the process documents (agents and humans) will read a stricter requirement for
+  runtime- or security-affecting one-line config edits; everything else routes exactly as
+  before.
